## What

**File ▸ Import Keywords…** and **File ▸ Export Keywords…** read and write keyword lists in Lightroom Classic's text format:

- one keyword per line, one tab per level of nesting;
- `{synonym}` lines under a keyword;
- `[name]` for a keyword left out of export.

Other apps use the same format:

- **Capture One** reads and writes it.
- **darktable** and **Adobe Bridge** use it.
- **Photo Supreme**'s *Formatted Vocabulary File* (`.utf8`, CRLF line ends, bracketed top category) is the same format. I checked this against a real 118-entry export, which round-trips. The file isn't committed.

### Import (`keyword.import {path | text}` → `{added, updated}`)
- **Adds:** keywords the library doesn't have are added with the list's attributes, spelled the way the library already spells any levels it has. Keywords it has keep their own attributes and gain the list's synonyms.
- **One undo step:** the whole import commits as a single batch.
- **Fast:** each lookup uses an index built once. A 40,000-line list against a 20,000-photo library imports in about a second; it took nearly two minutes before.
- **Accepts:**
  - Windows, Unix and classic Mac line ends;
  - a UTF-8 byte-order mark;
  - blank lines;
  - the same keyword repeated in another case (the first spelling wins).
- **Refuses, naming the line:**
  - space indentation;
  - control characters;
  - `|` in a name or synonym;
  - a synonym with no keyword above it;
  - an indent that skips a level;
  - more than 64 levels.
- **Other refusals:** a UTF-16 file is told to save as UTF-8. Files and `text` over 16 MB are refused.

### Export (`keyword.export {path?}` → `{keywords, captureOneRefuses, unwritable, path | text}`)
- **Always reads back:** anything the format can't hold is left out and listed in `…

**Why:** a slider drag or zoom that feels choppy gives no clue whether the time goes to the UI thread, to rendering, or to waiting on the GPU. This display answers that on screen. It's what found and measured the frame drops in #1240 and the overlapping renders fixed in #1302.

**What:** a debugging toggle, `view.frameRate` (`{show?: bool}`, toggles without parameters). It's a checked item in a new **Help ▸ Debug** submenu, so it's also in the command palette, the control channel and MCP. `LIGHTCRAFT_FPS=1` starts the app with it on, and it's never saved to preferences. In the window's top-right corner it shows:
- FPS now, the average, and the frames over 20 ms in the last second;
- the worst frame split into the UI thread's CPU time (layout, painting, texture uploads; eframe's `cpu_usage`) and the time spent waiting (vsync, or the GPU);
- the latest whole-frame and zoom-window render times, the render queue (queued / running / per second), and renders thrown away as superseded (new `Renderer::discarded`);
- the GPU adapter (or why it's off) and the renderer's GPU memory;
- a graph of the last ~240 frames: blue = UI CPU, green/orange/red = waiting, a white tick where a render arrived, lines at 60 and 30 fps.

While it shows, the window repaints continuously, so the rate is the one a drag gets. Once a second it logs the same numbers (`fps-hud: …`), so a scripted drag (`ui.dragWidget`) can be measured from the log; the A/B runs in #1240 and #1302 used this.

`Perf::host_frame_ms` is added exactly as #1009 adds it, so the two merge cleanly in either order.

