## What happens today
FreeBSD aarch64 was dropped from releases because the emulated aarch64 VM took over 5 hours. storytold/craft-common-actions#50 now cross-compiles aarch64 inside the KVM-accelerated x86_64 FreeBSD VM, opt-in through the `freebsd-architectures` input of `release.yml`. Its cross toolchain step exports `CARGO_BUILD_TARGET=aarch64-unknown-freebsd` before `packaging/freebsd/package.sh` runs. With aarch64 turned on, this app's script would:
- name the archive after `uname -m`, which is always amd64 in the guest, so the release job fails its archive-name check;
- read the binaries from `$CARGO_TARGET_DIR/release`, but cargo writes a cross build to `$CARGO_TARGET_DIR/aarch64-unknown-freebsd/release`;
- run the packaged binary with `--version`, and an aarch64 binary cannot run in the x86_64 guest (`Exec format error`, exit 126), which stops the script under `set -e`.

## Change
`packaging/freebsd/package.sh` only:
- the arch comes from `CARGO_BUILD_TARGET` when it is set (`aarch64-unknown-freebsd` → `aarch64`, `x86_64-unknown-freebsd` → `x86_64`), else from `uname -m` as before;
- a target other than `aarch64-unknown-freebsd` or `x86_64-unknown-freebsd` (a Linux or macOS triple, a bare arch, another FreeBSD triple) is refused with exit 2, and the target is read before `uname -m`, so an unsupported host does not block a valid cross build;
- binaries are read from `$CARGO_TARGET_DIR/${CARGO_BUILD_TARGET:+$CARGO_BUILD_TARGET/}release`;
- the `--version` run is skipped, with a message, when the package is for another architecture than the machine; `file(1)` must then report each staged binary as a FreeBSD ELF executable for the target architecture (an executable or PIE; libraries, object files and core files are refused). A FreeBSD target built on another OS (say an x86_64 Mac with `x86_64-unknown-freebsd`) also takes this path, since matching architectures do not make the binary runnable t…

Fixes #2588.

The filter bar's three flag buttons were one-at-a-time: `Filter` had a single `flag: Option<Flag>`, so turning on Unflagged switched Picked off. In Lightroom Classic the flag buttons of the Attribute filter (and the Filmstrip's) are independent toggles, and any combination shows photos with any of the chosen flags: Picked + Unflagged is the usual culling pass @Janhouse describes, everything but the rejects. Its flag context menu names the same combinations ("Flagged and Unflagged Photos", "Unflagged and Rejected Photos", …).

This follows what the colour labels already do (`labels` next to `label`):
- `Filter` gets `flags: Vec<Flag>`, any of them matches; empty = no constraint, and it is left out when serialized, so saved filters, presets and smart albums written today read and write exactly as before;
- the single `flag` stays as it is for saved filters, smart albums (`album.createSmart {rules: {flag}}`), `flag:pick` in search, the Picks source and agents; with both set they AND, as `label` + `labels` do;
- the filter bar's Flag buttons toggle `flags` (and clear an old `flag`, folding it into the list first, as the label swatches do); the chip reads "Flag: pick or none" and its × clears both;
- `library.filter` documents `flags?` (and `album.createSmart` lists `flags` and `labels`); `"flags": null` reads as `[]`, the way `"flag": null` clears the single flag; `describe` says "flag pick or none".

Inside the Picks source the flag buttons narrow the source, as the Attribute filter narrows a collection in Lightroom: Unflagged there shows 0 of N (before, it was silently ignored).

Classic calls the pick filter "Flagged" (its "Flagged" filter preset "Displays photos with a Pick flag"); I kept LightCraft's existing "Picked" tooltip and didn't add new strings, so no catalog changes. Happy to rename it, or to add a "click more flags to show any of them" hint like the label one, if you'd like.

